AB  - Chlorantraniliprole is a widely used diamide insecticide, while entomopathogenic fungi, particularly Beauveria bassiana, offer a sustainable alternative for pest control and can help reduce dependency on chemical pesticides. Because these fungi and chlorantraniliprole frequently coexist in agricultural environments, it is crucial to understand how the insecticide may influence fungal physiology and metabolism. In this study we assessed the effects of chlorantraniliprole on B. bassiana and employed LC-MS based metabolomics and multivariate statistical analyses (PCA, PLS-DA and OPLS-DA) to characterize potential metabolic shifts. The results showed that chlorantraniliprole significantly inhibited mycelial growth, sporulation, and spore germination of B. bassiana. Metabolomic profiling identified 67 differential metabolites, with 22 up-regulated and 45 down-regulated. KEGG enrichment analysis revealed that these metabolites were mainly involved in key metabolic pathways such as amino acid biosynthesis and metabolism, nucleotide metabolism, butanoate metabolism, ABC transporters, and oxidative phosphorylation. These metabolic alterations may affect membrane lipid composition and permeability of B. bassiana, as well as its energy metabolism and biosynthetic metabolism. The findings of this study enhance our understanding of the molecular mechanisms underlying fungal responses to environmental stress and provide valuable insights for strain improvement through metabolic regulation
