AB  - Tuta absoluta is a devasting pest causing severe yield losses in most tomato-growing areas. Macrolophus pygmaeus is a generalist predator released in tomato crop to control T. absoluta. Entomopathogenic fungi are also used on tomato against other pests, but they can play a role in controlling T. absoluta. Therefore, the effects of two commercial products Naturalis(R) and Mycotal(R), based on the entomopathogenic fungi Beauveria bassiana and Akanthomyces muscarius, respectively, on T. absoluta and M. pygmaeus were assessed. In no-choice tests on leaflets, mortality was significantly higher on fungus-treated leaflets for 1st-2nd instar T. absoluta larvae, and not for 3rd-4th instar larvae and for both younger and older M. pygmaeus nymphs. Similarly, in no-choice tests on plants, development rate was significantly lower only for T. absoluta on plants treated with Mycotal(R). In two-choice tests, both pest and predator did not show any preference between fungus-treated and untreated plants. Following the laboratory results, Mycotal(R) was applied also in plastic tunnel cultivation of tomato under field conditions. The number of T. absoluta larvae was significantly lower on plants treated with Mycotal(R) than on untreated plants, while the presence of M. pygmaeus was not affected by the treatment. Therefore, the use of fungal products together with the predator appears to be a promising strategy for the control of T. absoluta on tomato crop.
