AB  - Verticillium wilt, caused by Verticillium dahliae Kleb., is a destructive vascular disease that is difficult to control using chemical fungicides and remains a major challenge in plant disease management. Microbial biocontrol represents a sustainable alternative, and the identification of highly antagonistic strains against V. dahliae is of considerable importance. In this study, a strain of Talaromyces purpureogenus TS, which demonstrated strong antagonistic and mycoparasitic activity against V. dahliae, was isolated from soil. Its cell-free supernatant inhibited the growth of V. dahliae by disrupting the cell membrane, suggesting potential disorders in material and energy metabolism, as indicated by transcriptomic analysis; but showed no significant effect on the tested strains of Fusarium graminearum, Fusarium oxysporum and Saccharomyces cerevisiae under the conditions tested. Furthermore, the cell-free supernatant also exhibited broad-spectrum antibacterial properties. Compared to the biocontrol fungus Beauveria bassiana, T. purpureogenus TS exhibited stronger antagonistic activity against V. dahliae, along with higher conidial yield and stress tolerance. Furthermore, pre-inoculating of cotton plants with T. purpureogenus TS reduced disease symptoms and fungal colonization in cotton plants, indicating a protective effect. These results are expected to deepen the understanding of the characteristics of of T. purpureogenus, more importantly, the obtained strain has the pote
